Some conversions can fail. Text might not contain a number and a `Float` might
not fit inside `Int`.

Use `cast!` with a compatible builtin `Error!` return path:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    parse_count |text String| -> Int, Error!:
        return cast! text
    ;
]

Use `catch` when this part of the program has a sensible fallback:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count Int = cast text catch:
        then 0
    ;
]

Choose one path. A cast cannot use `!` and `catch` at the same time.

The handler covers both the conversion and evaluation of its operand. Arithmetic
and builtin `Error!` calls inside that operand can share the handler:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    narrow |left U32, right U32| -> U8:
        value U8 = cast left + right catch then 0
        return value
    ;
]

This recovers addition overflow as well as a result that does not fit `U8`.
Parentheses are not required. A bound error name has type `Error`; typed errors
keep their original message and code. Custom errors cannot mix with these
built-in failures.

A catch does not make an unsupported conversion valid. Numeric work known to
be invalid still produces a compile-time diagnostic. See @../progress (Progress)
for current support.

Converting to a narrower binary float is not automatically a failure. It fails
only when the rounded result is not finite, so losing precision is not an error
by itself. Converting a float to an integer is different: it truncates toward
zero and then checks the integer's range, so a value outside that range fails.
